\title[A Domain-Parameterised Budget Law for Attention Key Retention]
{A Domain-Parameterised Budget Law for Attention Key Retention}

\subjclass[2020]{68T07, 06A15, 20N02}
\keywords{attention key retention, knee of a sweep, Galois connection, budget law,
$\Z$-torsor, envelope of affine functions, grid resolution, attention concentration}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Truncating an attention mechanism's key cache to its $k$ heaviest keys degrades next-token
accuracy; the \emph{knee} $k^*$ is the smallest budget on a measurement grid retaining a
fixed fraction of full accuracy. We study how $k^*$ depends jointly on corpus domain and
context length. First, the knee is the left adjoint of the accuracy curve: for monotone
sweeps, $k^*_{\mathrm{idx}} \le j \iff \tau \le \acc(j)$, from which we derive a
data-determination principle---a knee is fixed by exactly two readings, the last failing
and the first passing grid index. Applied to a measured sweep over Python source this
yields $k^*=12$ at context $512$ and $k^*=16$ at context $1024$, against prose values $16$
and $20$: a constant shift of $-4$ keys, exactly one grid step, at both contexts. Second,
we introduce the budget law $k^*(\text{domain},d) = \base(\text{domain}) + \inc \cdot d$
with $d$ the number of context doublings, and prove affine rigidity, non-identifiability
from one context, and that a context-independent inter-domain shift is \emph{equivalent} to
a shared increment; unequal increments force a computable crossover. Third, we show the
domain axis at a fixed scale is a $\Z$-torsor: only differences of bases are observable and
they compose additively along chains. Inside a torsor fibre the mixed-workload envelope is
the join and is again a law with the largest base; outside it, if two domains have unequal
increments and crossing bases then \emph{no} budget law computes their envelope. Fourth,
every pair (full accuracy, knee) is realised by an actual domain, so no function of accuracy
predicts the knee; what the knee does certify is concentration---a knee of $k$ at tolerance
$\tau$ forces a single key of mass exceeding $\tau/(k+1)$. Finally, a geometric-decay rival
that divides rather than shifts the knee reproduces all four measured cells exactly, an
identifiability failure we prove; but the ceiling bracket forces its exponent above $5/4$,
whence it predicts at most $23$ keys at context $4096$ where the additive law predicts
exactly $24$, and no exponent reconciles both.
\end{abstract}

