\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The sizing problem}
An autoregressive attention model consulting a history of $N$ tokens holds $N$ key/value
pairs in memory. Memory is linear in $N$; deployability is not. The standard mitigation is
\emph{key retention}: at each decoding step the model attends only to the $k$ keys carrying
the greatest attention mass. Retained accuracy---accuracy under truncation, normalised by
untruncated accuracy---rises steeply with $k$ and then flattens. The location of the bend,
the \emph{knee}, is what a deployment must know.

\subsection{What is at stake}
A knee is a single integer, and single integers invite two errors: treating $k^*$ as a
fitted curve parameter when it is determined by two readings, and treating $k^*$ as a scalar
summary of \emph{difficulty}. This paper dismantles both. The vehicle is a domain jump:
holding the harness, acceptance bar, grid, window count and split protocol byte-identical,
an English prose corpus is replaced by Python source (ten files of the CPython standard
library) and the sweep re-run at two context lengths.

\subsection{The measurement}
