\toprule
budget $k$ & $4$ & $8$ & $12$ & $16$ & $20$ & $24$ \\
\midrule
ctx $512$  & $0.930$ & $0.969$ & $\mathbf{0.981}$ & $0.987$ & $0.988$ & $0.989$ \\
ctx $1024$ & --- & $0.960$ & $0.976$ & $\mathbf{0.981}$ & $0.986$ & $0.987$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Full untruncated accuracy on the code corpus is $0.6296$ at context $512$ and $0.6520$ at
context $1024$, in both cases above the prose figure. The resulting knees, against the prose
reference:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
context & code $k^*$ & prose $k^*$ & shift \\
\midrule
$512$  & $12$ & $16$ & $-4$ \\
$1024$ & $16$ & $20$ & $-4$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Three horns were registered in advance. \textbf{P1}---knees transfer across the domain jump
to within one grid step---\emph{confirmed}. \textbf{P2}---the shift is exactly one grid step
at both contexts---\emph{confirmed}. \textbf{P3}---the more accurately predicted domain
requires at least as many keys---\emph{refuted}: code is the more accurately predicted
corpus and needs strictly fewer keys.

\section{The knee as an adjoint}

A \emph{sweep} is a function $\acc : \N \to \Q$ assigning to grid index $j$ the retained
accuracy at budget $g \cdot j$, where $g$ is the grid step; the \emph{bar} is a rational
$\tau$.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{knee index} and \emph{knee budget} are
\[
k^*_{\mathrm{idx}}(\acc,\tau) = \inf\{\, j \in \N : \tau \le \acc(j) \,\},
\qquad
k^*(g,\acc,\tau) = g\, k^*_{\mathrm{idx}}(\acc,\tau).
\]
\end{definition}

Two facts hold with no hypotheses: any passing index bounds the knee above, and every index
strictly below the knee fails the bar.

\begin{theorem}[Galois connection]\label{thm:galois}
Let $\acc$ be monotone and suppose some index passes the bar. Then for every $k$,
\[
k^*_{\mathrm{idx}}(\acc,\tau) \le k \iff \tau \le \acc(k).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) The passing set is a nonempty set of naturals, so its infimum is a member;
hence the knee itself passes, and monotonicity transports this to any larger $k$.
($\Leftarrow$) Immediate from the infimum bound.
\end{proof}

Thus $k^*_{\mathrm{idx}}(-,\tau)$ is left adjoint to $\acc$. Two consequences follow
formally.

\begin{corollary}
If $\tau \le \sigma$ and some index passes $\sigma$ then
$k^*_{\mathrm{idx}}(\acc,\tau) \le k^*_{\mathrm{idx}}(\acc,\sigma)$: the knee is monotone in
the bar.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
If $\acc(j) \le \acc'(j)$ for all $j$ and some index passes for $\acc$, then
$k^*_{\mathrm{idx}}(\acc',\tau) \le k^*_{\mathrm{idx}}(\acc,\tau)$: a uniformly
better-retaining domain never needs more keys.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Data determination]\label{thm:bracket}
Let $\acc$ be monotone and let $j$ satisfy $\acc(j) < \tau \le \acc(j+1)$. Then
$k^*_{\mathrm{idx}}(\acc,\tau) = j+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The upper bound is the infimum bound at $j+1$. If $k^*_{\mathrm{idx}} \le j$, then
Theorem~\ref{thm:galois} gives $\tau \le \acc(j)$, contradicting the bracket.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bracket congruence]
Two monotone sweeps with the same bracketing pair have the same knee, regardless of their
values at every other index.
\end{corollary}

A knee claim therefore commits to exactly two numbers: the last failure and the first
success. Every other point in a sweep corroborates monotonicity but is not evidence for the
knee. Accordingly the results below are stated for \emph{every} monotone sweep agreeing with
the measurement at the bracketing pair.

\section{The measurement, formalised}

Fix grid step $g=4$ and bar $\tau = 0.98$.

\begin{theorem}[Code at context $512$]
Every monotone sweep with $\acc(2) = 0.969$ and $\acc(3) = 0.981$ has knee index $3$, i.e.\
$k^* = 12$ keys.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$0.969 < 0.98 \le 0.981$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:bracket} at $j=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Code at context $1024$]
Every monotone sweep with $\acc(3) = 0.976$ and $\acc(4) = 0.981$ has knee index $4$, i.e.\
$k^* = 16$ keys.
\end{theorem}

The hypotheses are not vacuous: the measured tables
\[
S_{512} = (0,\,0.930,\,0.969,\,0.981,\,0.987,\,0.988,\,0.989,\dots),
\]
\[
S_{1024} = (0,\,0,\,0.960,\,0.976,\,0.981,\,0.986,\,0.987,\dots)
\]
are monotone with constant tails, so the knees $12$ and $16$ are attained by actual curves;
and all sub-knee readings genuinely fail the bar.

\begin{remark}
The $k=4$ cell at context $1024$ was not swept. It cannot matter: monotonicity places it
below the $k=8$ reading of $0.960 < \tau$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:bracket} no unmeasured
index can enter a knee determined by a bracket.
\end{remark}

\section{Budget laws}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{budget law} is a pair $L = (b,c) \in \Z^2$ with \emph{base} $b$ and \emph{increment}
$c$, evaluated on the number $d$ of context doublings by $L(d) = b + c\,d$. By convention
$d=0$ is context $512$, so $d=1,2,3$ are $1024, 2048, 4096$.
\end{definition}

Immediately $L(0)=b$, $L(d+1)=L(d)+c$, and $L$ is monotone in $d$ exactly when $c \ge 0$.

\begin{theorem}[Affine rigidity]\label{thm:rigidity}
Two budget laws agreeing at two distinct contexts $d \ne e$ are equal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Subtracting the agreement equations gives $(c_A - c_B)(d-e) = 0$ over $\Z$; as $d \ne e$ and
$\Z$ is a domain, $c_A = c_B$, whence $b_A = b_B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[One context never identifies]
For every law $L=(b,c)$ and every $d$, the law $L' = (b-d,\,c+1)$ differs from $L$ yet
satisfies $L'(d) = L(d)$.
\end{proposition}

This is why the round had to be run at two context lengths.

\begin{theorem}[Constant shift $\iff$ shared increment]\label{thm:shiftinc}
For budget laws $A,B$,
\[
\big(\exists s \in \Z\ \forall d,\ A(d)-B(d) = s\big) \iff c_A = c_B .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) The increments cancel and the difference is $b_A-b_B$ at every $d$.
($\Rightarrow$) Evaluating at $d=0$ and $d=1$ gives $b_A-b_B = s$ and
$(b_A+c_A)-(b_B+c_B) = s$; subtracting yields $c_A = c_B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
With a shared increment, $A(d)-B(d) = b_A - b_B$ for every $d$: the shift is a context-free
integer.
\end{corollary}

This is the structural content of horn P2: a shift measured to be the same at two contexts
is \emph{equivalent} to the factorisation ``increment depends only on scale, base only on
domain''. The alternative had teeth:

\begin{theorem}[Unequal increments force a crossover]\label{thm:crossover}
If $c_A < c_B$ then there is a computable $D \in \N$ with $A(d) < B(d)$ for all $d \ge D$;
one may take $D = (b_A - b_B)^+ + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $d \ge D$ we have $b_A - b_B < d$ and $c_B - c_A \ge 1$, hence
$b_A - b_B < d \le d(c_B-c_A)$, which rearranges to $A(d) < B(d)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The fit}

\begin{definition}
$\codeLaw = (12,4)$ and $\proseLaw = (16,4)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Fit and uniqueness]
$\codeLaw(0)=12$, $\codeLaw(1)=16$, $\proseLaw(0)=16$, $\proseLaw(1)=20$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity}, any law with the code readings equals $\codeLaw$, and any law
with the prose readings equals $\proseLaw$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The measured shift]
For all $d$, $\proseLaw(d) - \codeLaw(d) = 4 = g$. Consequently
$|\proseLaw(d)-\codeLaw(d)| \le g$ (horn P1), the laws never cross, and by
Theorem~\ref{thm:shiftinc} the increments coincide.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Consistency]
The knee budgets computed from the measured sweeps equal the fitted law's predictions:
$k^*(S_{512}) = \codeLaw(0)$ and $k^*(S_{1024}) = \codeLaw(1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Falsifiable extrapolation]\label{thm:pred4096}
$\codeLaw(3) = 24$ and $\proseLaw(3) = 28$: at context $4096$ the law predicts $24$ keys for
code and $28$ for prose.
\end{theorem}

\section{The domain axis is a $\Z$-torsor}

Fix an increment $c$ and let $\mathcal{F}_c = \{L : c_L = c\}$ be the \emph{fibre} of laws
with that scale behaviour.

\begin{definition}
For $t \in \Z$, set $\tr_t(b,c) = (b+t,\,c)$.
\end{definition}

Translation preserves the increment, satisfies $\tr_t(L)(d) = L(d)+t$ for every $d$, and
composes: $\tr_s \circ \tr_t = \tr_{s+t}$ with $\tr_0 = \mathrm{id}$. So $\Z$ acts on
$\mathcal{F}_c$.

\begin{theorem}[Free and transitive]\label{thm:torsor}
If $c_A = c_B$ then there is a \emph{unique} $t \in \Z$ with $\tr_t(A) = B$, namely
$t = b_B - b_A$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Existence is direct; for uniqueness, comparing bases in $\tr_t(A) = B$ gives $b_A + t = b_B$.
\end{proof}

Each fibre is therefore a $\Z$-torsor. Choosing a base as origin gives a bijection
$\mathcal{F}_c \cong \Z$, $L \mapsto b_L$: a coordinate chart, not a canonical
identification. A domain, at fixed scale, is exactly one integer of information.

\begin{definition}
$\shift(A,B) = b_B - b_A$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Torsor arithmetic]\label{thm:cocycle}
$\shift(A,A) = 0$, $\shift(B,A) = -\shift(A,B)$, and the cocycle law holds:
\[
\shift(A,B) + \shift(B,C) = \shift(A,C).
\]
Moreover, when $c_A = c_B$, $\shift(A,B) = B(d) - A(d)$ for every $d$.
\end{theorem}

The cocycle law says a ladder of domains is generated by its consecutive gaps.

\begin{theorem}[Only differences are observable]\label{thm:observable}
For every $t \in \Z$ and all laws $A,B$ in a common fibre,
$\shift(\tr_t A, \tr_t B) = \shift(A,B)$. Moreover, for any target $\beta$ there is a
translation $t$ making $\tr_t A$ have base $\beta$ while leaving all shifts unchanged.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(b_B+t)-(b_A+t) = b_B-b_A$; for the second claim take $t = \beta - b_A$.
\end{proof}

The origin of the domain axis is pure convention. The experiment measures
$\shift(\text{code},\text{prose}) = 4$; it does \emph{not} measure $12$ and $16$ separately,
and no experiment of this design could.

\begin{theorem}[The round-21 domain axis]
$c_{\codeLaw} = c_{\proseLaw}$; there is a unique $t$ with $\tr_t(\codeLaw) = \proseLaw$;
$\shift(\codeLaw,\proseLaw) = 4 = g$; and this shift is invariant under common re-basing. A
hypothetical third domain at base $8$ extends the ladder consistently:
$\shift((8,4),\codeLaw) + \shift(\codeLaw,\proseLaw) = 8$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Mixed workloads: the envelope}

Deployments serve heterogeneous traffic; the operational object is the envelope
$E(d) = \max_i L_i(d)$.

\subsection{Inside a fibre: the envelope is the join}

\begin{theorem}[Order is order of bases]
If $c_A = c_B$ then $\big(\forall d,\ A(d) \le B(d)\big) \iff b_A \le b_B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Envelope law]\label{thm:envelope}
For a nonempty finite family $(b_i)_{i \in S}$ with common increment $c$,
\[
\max_{i \in S}\big(b_i + c\,d\big) = \Big(\max_{i \in S} b_i\Big) + c\,d,
\]
so the envelope is again a budget law, with base the largest base.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Translation $x \mapsto x + cd$ is an order isomorphism of $\Z$ and hence commutes with finite
suprema.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Deployment rule]\label{cor:deploy}
Size the cache by the largest-base domain present. For prose/code,
$\max(\codeLaw(d),\proseLaw(d)) = \proseLaw(d)$ at every $d$, and sizing by code
under-provisions the mixture by exactly one grid step,
$\max(\codeLaw(d),\proseLaw(d)) - \codeLaw(d) = 4$, independently of context.
\end{corollary}

Corollary~\ref{cor:deploy} is a falsifiable claim about corpus mixing: the base of a mixture
is the \emph{maximum} of the constituent bases, never a weighted mean.

\subsection{Outside a fibre: the envelope is not a law}

\begin{theorem}[No envelope law]\label{thm:nolaw}
Let $A,B$ be budget laws with $c_A < c_B$ and $b_B < b_A$ (unequal increments, crossing
bases). Then there is \emph{no} budget law $C$ with $C(d) = \max(A(d),B(d))$ for all
$d \in \N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:crossover} there is $D$ with $A(d) < B(d)$ for all $d \ge D$, so any such
$C$ satisfies $C(d) = B(d)$ for $d \ge D$, in particular at the distinct contexts $D$ and
$D+1$. Affine rigidity (Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity}) forces $C = B$, whence $C(0) = b_B$;
but $\max(A(0),B(0)) = b_A > b_B$. Contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Taking $A = (16,2)$ and $B = (12,6)$ satisfies both hypotheses, so no budget law computes
$\max(16+2d,\ 12+6d)$.
\end{corollary}

That envelope reads $16, 18, 24, 30, 36, \dots$: a genuine kink at $d=1$ that no
two-parameter affine formula can straighten. Single-formula cache sizing for a mixed
workload is a privilege, not a default---exactly what the measured constancy of the $-4$
shift buys.

\section{Accuracy is orthogonal to the knee}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{measured domain} is a pair $D = (a,P)$ where $a \in \Q$ is full untruncated accuracy
and $P$ is an \emph{attention profile}: a nondecreasing cumulative mass function
$\cum_P : \N \to \Q$ with $\cum_P(0)=0$, the mass of the $j$-th key being
$\cum_P(j+1)-\cum_P(j)$. The \emph{knee at tolerance $\tau$} is the least $k$ with
$\tau \le \cum_P(k)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Decoupling]\label{thm:decouple}
For every $0 < \tau < 1$, every accuracy value $a$ and every $k \ge 1$, there is a measured
domain with full accuracy exactly $a$ and knee exactly $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take the uniform profile on $k$ keys: mass $\tau/k$ on each of the first $k$ keys, so
$\cum(j) = \tau\min(j,k)/k$. Then $\cum(j) < \tau$ for $j<k$ and $\cum(k)=\tau$; the
accuracy coordinate is a free label.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No accuracy functional]
For $0 < \tau < 1$ there is no $g : \Q \to \N$ with $\knee_\tau(D) = g(\fullAcc(D))$ for all
measured domains $D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:decouple} choose $D,E$ both with accuracy $\tfrac12$ and knees $1$ and
$2$; then $g(\tfrac12)$ is both $1$ and $2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Both directions occur]
For $0<\tau<1$ there exist measured domains $D,E$ with $\fullAcc(E) < \fullAcc(D)$ and
$\knee_\tau(D) < \knee_\tau(E)$---the observed code/prose pattern---and also domains
realising the reverse. Hence no monotone weakening of P3 survives. Explicit witnesses:
accuracies $\tfrac23$ and $\tfrac13$ with knees $(1,2)$ and with knees $(2,1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}
There exist measured domains with full accuracy $0.6296$ and knee $12$, and with full
accuracy $0.6520$ and knee $16$: the recorded code cells at contexts $512$ and $1024$ lie
inside the framework.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{What the base does measure: concentration}

\begin{theorem}[A knee certifies a heavy key]\label{thm:concentration}
Let $P$ be an attention profile with $0<\tau<1$ and $\knee_\tau(P) = k$. Then
\[
\exists j,\qquad \frac{\tau}{k+1} < \cum_P(j+1) - \cum_P(j).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Contrapositive of the concentration bound: if every key's mass is at most $m>0$, then
accumulating $\tau$ requires at least $\tau/m$ keys, i.e.\ $\knee_\tau(P) \ge \tau/m$.
Putting $m = \tau/(k+1)$ would give $k \ge k+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
With code meeting tolerance $\tau$ at $12$ keys, the code profile contains a key of mass
exceeding $\tau/13$; in particular it cannot be flat at level $\tau/13$. For prose, with
knee $16$, the analogous guarantee reaches only $\tau/17$.
\end{corollary}

The $-4$ shift is therefore a measurable statement about the \emph{shape} of code
attention---code has a heavier head---and not about its difficulty. This matches the
qualitative character of source code: references are sharp (an identifier binds at one line,
a delimiter matches one partner, a call resolves to one definition), whereas prose reference
is diffuse.

\section{The adversarial round: additive shift or rescaled decay?}

\subsection{Geometric profiles and the continuous knee}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{geometric profile} with decay rate $r \in (0,1)$ has residual mass $r^k$ after $k$
keys. Given residual tolerance $\rho = 1-\tau \in (0,1)$, its knee is
$\geomKnee(r,\rho) = \inf\{k \in \N : r^k \le \rho\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact knee]\label{thm:geomknee}
For $0<r<1$ and $\rho>0$,
$\displaystyle \geomKnee(r,\rho) = \left\lceil \frac{\log\rho}{\log r} \right\rceil$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $\log r < 0$, $r^k \le \rho \iff k\log r \le \log\rho \iff X \le k$ where
$X = \log\rho/\log r$. The least natural $k$ with $X \le k$ is $\lceil X\rceil$.
\end{proof}

Call $X = \log\rho/\log r$ the \emph{continuous knee}: the real number of which the measured
integer is the ceiling.

\begin{theorem}[Rescaling divides the knee]
For $a>0$, $\geomKnee(r^a,\rho) = \lceil X/a \rceil$ with $X = \log\rho/\log r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\log(r^a) = a\log r$, so the defining quotient is divided by $a$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:geomknee} to $r^a \in (0,1)$.
\end{proof}

Thus ``the same attention shape, decaying $a$ times faster'' acts \emph{multiplicatively} on
the knee where the budget law acts additively.

\begin{proposition}[The model is nonempty]
Every $X>0$ is the continuous knee of an honest geometric profile: take $r=e^{-1}$ and
$\rho=e^{-X}$, both in $(0,1)$, giving $\geomKnee(r,\rho) = \lceil X\rceil$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Both mechanisms fit all four cells}

\begin{lemma}[Ceiling bracket]\label{lem:bracket}
If $\lceil X\rceil = n$ then $X \le n$; and if in addition $n \ge 1$ then $n-1 < X$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Identifiability failure]\label{thm:identif}
Both mechanisms reproduce the four measured knees exactly. Additive:
$\proseLaw(0)=16$, $\proseLaw(1)=20$, $\codeLaw(0)=12$, $\codeLaw(1)=16$. Rescaling: with
$a = 251/200 = 1.255$ and prose continuous knees $X_0 = 15.05$, $X_1 = 20$,
\[
\lceil X_0\rceil = 16,\quad \lceil X_0/a\rceil = 12,\quad
\lceil X_1\rceil = 20,\quad \lceil X_1/a\rceil = 16 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$15.05/1.255 = 11.99\ldots \in (11,12]$ and $20/1.255 = 15.93\ldots \in (15,16]$; the
required geometric profiles exist by the preceding proposition.
\end{proof}

Two contexts therefore cannot distinguish an additive base shift from a rescaling of the
attention decay rate. We state this as the honest limit of the round rather than as a
caveat.

\subsection{The discriminating experiment}

\begin{theorem}[The $512$ cell forces $a>5/4$]\label{thm:afive}
If $a>0$, $\lceil X\rceil = 16$ and $\lceil X/a\rceil = 12$, then $a > 5/4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:bracket} gives $X > 15$ and $X/a \le 12$, hence $X \le 12a$; so $15 < 12a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rescaling predicts at most $23$ at ctx $4096$]
If $a > 5/4$ and $\lceil X_3\rceil = 28$ then $\lceil X_3/a\rceil \le 23$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$X_3 \le 28$ and $a > 5/4$ give $X_3/a < 28\cdot\tfrac45 = 22.4 \le 23$; take ceilings.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The separation]
The additive law predicts exactly $24$ keys for code at context $4096$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:pred4096}). Every decay-rescaling model reproducing the measured $512$
cell predicts strictly fewer. A single measurement at context $4096$ separates the two
mechanisms.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The exponent window closes]\label{thm:window}
There is no $a>0$ and no $X_0,X_3$ with $\lceil X_0\rceil = 16$, $\lceil X_0/a\rceil = 12$,
$\lceil X_3\rceil = 28$ and $\lceil X_3/a\rceil = 24$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first pair forces $a > 5/4$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:afive}). The last pair forces $X_3/a > 23$
while $X_3 \le 28$, hence $23a < 28$, i.e.\ $a < 28/23 \approx 1.2174 < 1.25$.
\end{proof}

So a $4096$ reading of $24$ kills the multiplicative mechanism outright; any reading $\le 23$
kills the additive law.

\begin{theorem}[The separation is sharp]
Context $4096$ is the first that decides. The single witness $a = 251/200$ also reproduces
the additive prediction at context $2048$: $\codeLaw(2) = 20$, and with prose continuous knee
$24$ one has $\lceil 24\rceil = 24$ and $\lceil 24/a\rceil = 20$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The general statement behind these results is that the admissible exponent window is
$\bigcap_d \big((P_d-1)/C_d,\ P_d/(C_d-1)\big)$, where $P_d, C_d$ are the prose and code
readings at doubling $d$. The additive and multiplicative branches are numerically
indistinguishable exactly while this window is nonempty; it becomes empty at $d=3$.
\end{remark}

\section{Grid resolution}

\begin{definition}
For grid step $g \ge 1$, $\roundUp{g}{k} = g\lceil k/g\rceil$, the least multiple of $g$ at or
above $k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
If $g \mid k$ then $\roundUp{g}{k} = k$: readings already on the grid are undistorted.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[A coarse grid hides the shift]\label{thm:coarse}
$\roundUp{8}{12} = \roundUp{8}{16} = 16$. On a grid of step $8$ the code knee $12$ and the
prose knee $16$ are the same reading and the entire effect vanishes without trace. On the
step-$4$ grid, $\roundUp{4}{12} = 12 \ne 16 = \roundUp{4}{16}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Aliasing criterion]
Two knees are indistinguishable on a grid of step $g$ exactly when they lie in the same
$g$-cell, i.e.\ when $\lceil a/g\rceil = \lceil b/g\rceil$.
\end{proposition}

A domain effect of size $s$ is thus observable only on grids finer than $s$. The binding
constraint on what a limited-memory experiment can detect is the grid step---not the number
of evaluation windows, the corpus size, or the tightness of the bar.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Bracketed knee extraction}
Given a monotone sweep on a grid of step $g$ and a bar $\tau$, scan for the unique index $j$
with $\acc(j) < \tau \le \acc(j+1)$ and return $g(j+1)$ together with the bracketing pair as
a certificate. Complexity $O(n)$, or $O(\log n)$ by binary search---valid precisely because
Theorem~\ref{thm:galois} makes the passing set upward closed. By Theorem~\ref{thm:bracket}
the certificate is a complete justification of the reading.

\subsection{Two-point law identification and envelope synthesis}
Given readings at distinct doublings $d \ne e$, solve $c = (k_e-k_d)/(e-d)$ and
$b = k_d - c\,d$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity} this is the unique law, and integrality of
$c$ is a testable consistency condition. For a family of laws, test whether all increments
agree; if so return the envelope law $(\max_i b_i,\,c)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:envelope}; if not,
report that the envelope is not a law whenever some pair has unequal increments with crossing
bases (Theorem~\ref{thm:nolaw}) and return the pointwise envelope. Complexity $O(|S|)$.

\subsection{Mechanism discrimination via the exponent window}
Given prose and code readings $(P_d,C_d)$, form
$I_d = \big((P_d-1)/C_d,\ P_d/(C_d-1)\big)$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:bracket} and intersect. A
nonempty intersection means the multiplicative mechanism survives; an empty one excludes it.
On the measured cells the intersection is nonempty (it contains $1.255$); adjoining the
additive prediction at $d=3$ empties it. Complexity linear in the number of cells.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What was actually measured}
The invariant content of the round is a single integer:
$\shift(\text{code},\text{prose}) = 4$. Theorem~\ref{thm:observable} shows this is not a
rhetorical flourish---any experiment of this design measures a torsor element, and absolute
bases are chart-dependent. Domain taxonomies for cache sizing should therefore be published
as tables of consecutive gaps, which compose by Theorem~\ref{thm:cocycle}, rather than as
tables of absolute budgets, which do not transport across changes of reference context.

\subsection{Why the constancy is the result}
Theorem~\ref{thm:shiftinc} converts the repeated $-4$ into a factorisation claim: base
depends only on domain, increment only on scale. Theorem~\ref{thm:crossover} shows the
alternative was live. The value $-4$ tells you about two corpora; the \emph{constancy} of
$-4$ tells you the model has two separable parameters, and that is the transportable
finding.

\subsection{The cost of the assumption}
Theorem~\ref{thm:nolaw} is the sharpest statement here, and it is negative. The comfortable
single-formula sizing rule is not robust: the moment two domains differ in increment and
cross in base, the envelope leaves the model class entirely. Deployments mixing domains of
genuinely different scale behaviour should verify the shared increment before applying
Corollary~\ref{cor:deploy}, and should expect a kinked envelope otherwise.

\subsection{Difficulty is the wrong intuition}
The decoupling results close off an entire class of would-be explanations: whatever governs
the base, it is not predictive difficulty. Theorem~\ref{thm:concentration} supplies the right
intuition---concentration. A small base \emph{is} a heavy attention head, connecting a
downstream budget reading to an upstream shape property measurable from attention statistics
with no downstream task at all.

\subsection{Limitations}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{One programming language.} The code corpus is Python only; nothing shows the
base is a property of ``code'' rather than of Python.
\item \textbf{Single-repository source.} Ten files from one standard library; stylistic
homogeneity is a plausible confound.
\item \textbf{Two contexts.} Twenty-four evaluation windows per cell at contexts $512$ and
$1024$ only. Two points identify an affine law but cannot distinguish it from the
multiplicative rival (Theorem~\ref{thm:identif})---a limitation we prove rather than assert.
\item \textbf{One grid step.} The step-$4$ grid resolves the effect
(Theorem~\ref{thm:coarse}), but a shift of size $1$, $2$ or $3$ would be invisible to it.
\item \textbf{One acceptance bar.} All readings at $\tau = 0.98$ of full accuracy;
monotonicity of the knee in the bar says nothing about how the \emph{shift} moves.
\end{enumerate}
Against these, the design controls are exact: byte-identical harness except for the text, an
exact rather than statistical acceptance gate, held-out per-corpus splits, and a
deterministic pipeline.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{D1. Additive base versus multiplicative decay: the $4096$ decision.} The additive and
decay-rescaling laws are not alternative fits of one curve---they are the linear and the
ceiling-of-a-quotient branches of a single two-parameter family, numerically
indistinguishable exactly while the admissible exponent window
$\bigcap_d \big((P_d-1)/C_d,\ P_d/(C_d-1)\big)$ stays nonempty; that window is empty from
$d=3$ on. Since Theorem~\ref{thm:window} is established, the experiment is fully
pre-registered: a $4096$ code reading of $24$ kills the multiplicative mechanism, and any
reading $\le 23$ kills the additive law.

\textbf{D2. The base as a concentration invariant.} Theorem~\ref{thm:concentration} turns a
knee reading into a lower bound on the heaviest key's mass, suggesting the base of a domain
is exactly the reciprocal of its asymptotic top-key mass---a shape invariant measurable
without any downstream task. The inequality holds in one direction and the uniform profile
shows it is tight; a matching upper bound for realistic heavy-tailed profiles is missing.

\textbf{D3. Base additivity under corpus mixing.} Theorem~\ref{thm:envelope} makes the mixed
budget the join of the individual bases, predicting that a mixed corpus has base equal to the
maximum of its constituents and never their weighted mean---a sharp, easily falsified claim
about interleaved prose/code streams, testable by a single interleaved-corpus sweep. The
shared increment is load-bearing: Theorem~\ref{thm:nolaw} shows that with unequal increments
and crossing bases the envelope is not a budget law at all.

\textbf{D4. Resolution theory of knee grids.} A domain effect of size $s$ is observable only
on grids finer than $s$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:coarse} shows the whole effect disappears at
step $8$. The grid step, not the number of windows, binds what a limited-memory experiment
can detect; a systematic account of the trade-off between grid refinement and window count is
the natural next piece of methodology.

\textbf{Further axes.} Mathematical and non-English natural-language domains, to test whether
the base ladder extends beyond prose/code; increments measured directly at context $4096$; a
hybrid retention policy combining top-$k$ probing with a recency window, evaluated on code;
and a larger-model cell under quantised offload, to test whether the base is a property of
the corpus or of the model.

\section{Conclusion}

A memory-budget table has been converted into a theory with definite structure. The knee is
an adjoint, hence determined by two readings rather than fitted. The budget factors as base
plus increment times doublings, and the observed constancy of the inter-domain shift is
\emph{equivalent} to that factorisation. The domain axis is a $\Z$-torsor, so only
differences of bases are observable and they compose additively along chains. Inside a scale
class the mixed-workload envelope is the join and is itself a law; outside it, no law
computes the envelope at all. The domain parameter is provably not a function of predictive
accuracy, and it lower-bounds the heaviest key's attention mass instead. And the one
mechanism that could have masqueraded as an additive shift is separated from it by a single,
pre-registered measurement whose outcome will kill one of the two.

Code needs four fewer keys than prose, at every context. That number is the whole of the
domain information at this scale---and it is a statement about the shape of attention, not
about the difficulty of the text.

